1
00:00:09,620 --> 00:00:14,890
Let's jump into TLS
and HTTPS this time.

2
00:00:16,490 --> 00:00:19,630
What are we addressing here?

3
00:00:19,630 --> 00:00:22,830
This is at the application
and transport layer.

4
00:00:22,830 --> 00:00:27,255
Without these, the
problem is TCP it

5
00:00:27,255 --> 00:00:29,690
provides in order
reliable stream

6
00:00:29,690 --> 00:00:32,495
between two processes,
so we learned that,

7
00:00:32,495 --> 00:00:35,510
and then HTTP provides

8
00:00:35,510 --> 00:00:38,850
an application
protocol to get and

9
00:00:38,850 --> 00:00:43,270
put web objects with the server.

10
00:00:43,270 --> 00:00:46,710
An attacker could pretend

11
00:00:46,710 --> 00:00:49,580
to be the bank in this example;

12
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:52,975
it could inspect
or modify traffic;

13
00:00:52,975 --> 00:00:57,855
it could snoop on Wi-Fi in
public space and so forth.

14
00:00:57,855 --> 00:01:00,115
Is this threat really real,

15
00:01:00,115 --> 00:01:03,590
though pretending to
be somebody else,

16
00:01:03,590 --> 00:01:08,890
a different website,
or modifying messages?

17
00:01:08,890 --> 00:01:10,490
Well, we saw part of that,

18
00:01:10,490 --> 00:01:12,790
but to show it again,

19
00:01:12,790 --> 00:01:15,650
what happens when,

20
00:01:15,650 --> 00:01:19,035
in my browser, I
go to chase.com?

21
00:01:19,035 --> 00:01:24,375
Well, a DNS query is performed
to look up chase.com,

22
00:01:24,375 --> 00:01:27,135
and that returns
on my IP address,

23
00:01:27,135 --> 00:01:29,750
and then I send messages to and

24
00:01:29,750 --> 00:01:33,800
from that IP address that
was returned from DNS.

25
00:01:36,050 --> 00:01:39,490
We've seen cases or how we might

26
00:01:39,490 --> 00:01:43,050
be able to modify
packets directly,

27
00:01:43,050 --> 00:01:47,185
or perhaps the DNS system

28
00:01:47,185 --> 00:01:50,950
responds with the
wrong IP address;

29
00:01:50,950 --> 00:01:56,995
it's also the case that BGP
routes could be hijacked,

30
00:01:56,995 --> 00:02:01,190
and so even if you're sending
to the correct IP address,

31
00:02:01,190 --> 00:02:04,170
it ends up at a
different destination.

32
00:02:05,560 --> 00:02:12,690
TLS is introduced as a layer
that provides security.

33
00:02:12,690 --> 00:02:14,690
It provides confidentiality,

34
00:02:14,690 --> 00:02:18,760
integrity, and authentication.

35
00:02:18,760 --> 00:02:23,040
It's shown here in the middle
when combined with HTTP;

36
00:02:23,040 --> 00:02:27,190
that is what HTTPS is.

37
00:02:27,350 --> 00:02:30,030
Now, on the right-hand side,

38
00:02:30,030 --> 00:02:34,230
looking forward, we
didn't go over quick,

39
00:02:34,230 --> 00:02:36,290
so I won't discuss that here,

40
00:02:36,290 --> 00:02:40,110
but that incorporates a
lot of the capabilities

41
00:02:40,110 --> 00:02:46,420
of TLS into that in
this next generation.

42
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:51,150
Just to summarize the
problems of HTTP and

43
00:02:51,150 --> 00:02:53,450
TCP when used as

44
00:02:53,450 --> 00:02:56,380
the mechanism to
communicate with servers,

45
00:02:56,380 --> 00:03:00,195
the messages are
sent in plain text,

46
00:03:00,195 --> 00:03:03,620
the message integrity
is not insured,

47
00:03:03,620 --> 00:03:07,810
and we're not guaranteed
to be communicating with

48
00:03:07,810 --> 00:03:12,900
the server that we expect to be.

49
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:16,185
We've seen solutions
to all of these,

50
00:03:16,185 --> 00:03:20,655
so we can use
symmetric encryption;

51
00:03:20,655 --> 00:03:24,515
we can use message
authentication codes

52
00:03:24,515 --> 00:03:27,855
using a shared secret key,

53
00:03:27,855 --> 00:03:32,790
and these provide
confidentiality and integrity.

54
00:03:32,870 --> 00:03:36,150
But as we've pointed out before,

55
00:03:36,150 --> 00:03:39,610
how do we share a private key?

56
00:03:40,630 --> 00:03:42,920
There, we can use

57
00:03:42,920 --> 00:03:45,980
asymmetric encryption
to exchange the key.

58
00:03:45,980 --> 00:03:50,040
We can encrypt this shared
key, the session key,

59
00:03:50,040 --> 00:03:52,185
with the public key of a server,

60
00:03:52,185 --> 00:03:55,600
and the server decrypts
with their private key.

61
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:58,550
We could use Diffie-Hellman,

62
00:03:58,550 --> 00:04:01,935
all those different things.

63
00:04:01,935 --> 00:04:04,580
But in this case, how do we

64
00:04:04,580 --> 00:04:07,375
trust the public
key of the server,

65
00:04:07,375 --> 00:04:10,580
and in fact, how do we know
it's the actual server?

66
00:04:10,580 --> 00:04:13,820
This is where
digital certificates

67
00:04:13,820 --> 00:04:16,690
and certificate
authorities come in.

68
00:04:16,690 --> 00:04:18,720
This binds the identity,

69
00:04:18,720 --> 00:04:22,380
for example, the ownership
of a domain name,

70
00:04:22,380 --> 00:04:25,380
to some public key,

71
00:04:25,380 --> 00:04:29,875
and then that could be
signed by a trusted source.

72
00:04:29,875 --> 00:04:35,850
This provides the ability to
have authentication as well.

73
00:04:36,170 --> 00:04:40,470
One certificate authority that's

74
00:04:40,470 --> 00:04:44,650
highly used is called,
Let's Encrypt.

75
00:04:44,650 --> 00:04:51,805
This is an open system for
providing these certificates.

76
00:04:51,805 --> 00:04:55,140
How does it go about
verifying identity?

77
00:04:55,140 --> 00:04:57,780
We saw in the case of RPKI,

78
00:04:57,780 --> 00:05:01,725
there was this pre established
business relationship,

79
00:05:01,725 --> 00:05:05,370
the RARs, which served as

80
00:05:05,370 --> 00:05:10,155
the certificate
authorities in RPKI.

81
00:05:10,155 --> 00:05:15,360
In this case, there's not
that business relationship.

82
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:20,880
It's got a system where
it verifies the identity,

83
00:05:20,880 --> 00:05:27,330
namely the ownership of a
domain through a challenge.

84
00:05:27,330 --> 00:05:32,610
This could be whether it's
you provision a DNS record

85
00:05:32,610 --> 00:05:36,150
under say example.com if

86
00:05:36,150 --> 00:05:37,890
that's the domain that you're

87
00:05:37,890 --> 00:05:40,065
trying to issue a
certificate for,

88
00:05:40,065 --> 00:05:46,710
or provision in HTTP resource
under a well known URI.

89
00:05:46,710 --> 00:05:51,360
All right here you're
going to go request

90
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:57,705
a certificate and then the
Let's Encrypt system will say,

91
00:05:57,705 --> 00:05:59,445
great, why don't you put

92
00:05:59,445 --> 00:06:04,005
this value at this location

93
00:06:04,005 --> 00:06:06,405
for your website, this domain.

94
00:06:06,405 --> 00:06:10,470
Then their own servers
can then basically do

95
00:06:10,470 --> 00:06:17,340
a HTTP request to that
domain at that location.

96
00:06:17,340 --> 00:06:21,585
If that resource value
was provision there,

97
00:06:21,585 --> 00:06:24,225
then they've made
the assumption that

98
00:06:24,225 --> 00:06:27,435
you've proven that
you have ownership,

99
00:06:27,435 --> 00:06:30,510
you've proven
identity and so now

100
00:06:30,510 --> 00:06:33,600
we'll accept that this
is your public key

101
00:06:33,600 --> 00:06:36,570
for that domain and we're going

102
00:06:36,570 --> 00:06:40,630
to sign and create a
certificate for it.

103
00:06:41,990 --> 00:06:46,410
With that, now those
certificates are in place,

104
00:06:46,410 --> 00:06:51,720
we're going to have a web
server would go and do that.

105
00:06:51,720 --> 00:06:55,170
Now they have this
public key that

106
00:06:55,170 --> 00:06:56,640
they'll be able to share

107
00:06:56,640 --> 00:06:59,890
with anybody visiting
their website.

108
00:07:00,350 --> 00:07:03,225
The client would be able to say,

109
00:07:03,225 --> 00:07:05,430
oh, let's encrypt sign this.

110
00:07:05,430 --> 00:07:07,065
I know it's valid,

111
00:07:07,065 --> 00:07:09,090
it's tied to this identity.

112
00:07:09,090 --> 00:07:11,925
I know that's the
identity I'm looking for

113
00:07:11,925 --> 00:07:16,600
so it can trust that
communication now.

114
00:07:16,600 --> 00:07:20,880
There much like when
we talked about IP,

115
00:07:20,880 --> 00:07:24,570
there's this handshake
that needs to happen.

116
00:07:24,570 --> 00:07:29,265
This is the core of TLS.

117
00:07:29,265 --> 00:07:33,450
This happens after you
do the TCP connection.

118
00:07:33,450 --> 00:07:36,420
After you do that
three way handshake,

119
00:07:36,420 --> 00:07:39,765
it sets up the agreement
on the parameters,

120
00:07:39,765 --> 00:07:45,090
it authenticates the server
and it sets up a private key,

121
00:07:45,090 --> 00:07:48,450
then encrypted traffic
can exchange and you can

122
00:07:48,450 --> 00:07:54,610
do exchange of web
objects with the server.

123
00:07:54,680 --> 00:07:59,400
We're specifically
focusing on TLS 1.2,

124
00:07:59,400 --> 00:08:03,060
1.3 that is more concise.

125
00:08:03,060 --> 00:08:05,535
It reduces some of the messages,

126
00:08:05,535 --> 00:08:11,520
but this I feel
helps understand it,

127
00:08:11,520 --> 00:08:13,815
it keeps it a little more clear.

128
00:08:13,815 --> 00:08:17,775
We're going to use
1.2 as our example.

129
00:08:17,775 --> 00:08:21,885
The first message is from
the client to the server.

130
00:08:21,885 --> 00:08:22,950
It's a client.

131
00:08:22,950 --> 00:08:25,950
Hello. This tells the server of

132
00:08:25,950 --> 00:08:29,220
what the client can support
and what it prefers.

133
00:08:29,220 --> 00:08:35,070
The algorithms for encryption
that it supports, like DES,

134
00:08:35,070 --> 00:08:40,455
AES, its data integrity
algorithms like SHA,

135
00:08:40,455 --> 00:08:43,800
SHA1, MD5, and so forth.

136
00:08:43,800 --> 00:08:46,900
This is the parameters.

137
00:08:47,240 --> 00:08:49,530
The server picks with

138
00:08:49,530 --> 00:08:58,260
parameters and then tells
the client what it chose.

139
00:08:58,260 --> 00:09:01,660
It sends a server hello message.

140
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:04,910
The server then sends

141
00:09:04,910 --> 00:09:07,940
its digital certificate
that was signed by

142
00:09:07,940 --> 00:09:11,750
a certificate authority
and this is what

143
00:09:11,750 --> 00:09:16,565
binds that domain name to the
public key of the server.

144
00:09:16,565 --> 00:09:19,270
That's what the
certificate does.

145
00:09:19,270 --> 00:09:24,880
The client will have a root
certificate installed,

146
00:09:24,880 --> 00:09:27,715
or a bunch of root
certificates installed,

147
00:09:27,715 --> 00:09:31,780
and can verify the
server's certificate

148
00:09:31,780 --> 00:09:33,445
as long as it was signed

149
00:09:33,445 --> 00:09:36,175
by one of these
certificate authorities

150
00:09:36,175 --> 00:09:39,490
that was installed in,

151
00:09:39,490 --> 00:09:44,065
in this case, the
browser of the client.

152
00:09:44,065 --> 00:09:51,110
With this, it knows the
website it's expecting.

153
00:09:51,690 --> 00:09:54,970
The server then indicates it's

154
00:09:54,970 --> 00:09:59,180
done with its
handshake negotiation.

155
00:09:59,940 --> 00:10:07,270
At this point, the
client and the server,

156
00:10:07,270 --> 00:10:12,085
have the ability to
exchange a session key.

157
00:10:12,085 --> 00:10:16,750
We have the public key
of the server and so

158
00:10:16,750 --> 00:10:22,180
the client can initiate this.

159
00:10:22,180 --> 00:10:25,345
The client can pick
a session key,

160
00:10:25,345 --> 00:10:29,315
encrypt it with the
public key of the server,

161
00:10:29,315 --> 00:10:32,800
and send it over.

162
00:10:33,510 --> 00:10:38,545
Now that's part of the
client key exchange.

163
00:10:38,545 --> 00:10:41,230
Now the client can indicate that

164
00:10:41,230 --> 00:10:43,810
it now believes the
parameters are agreed

165
00:10:43,810 --> 00:10:50,090
upon and that the next
message will be encrypted.

166
00:10:51,780 --> 00:10:54,310
As part of that,

167
00:10:54,310 --> 00:10:58,975
it then sends the summary of
all messages to this point,

168
00:10:58,975 --> 00:11:01,930
ensuring the integrity of all of

169
00:11:01,930 --> 00:11:05,930
them and that none of them
have been tampered with.

170
00:11:06,840 --> 00:11:10,675
The server then also sends

171
00:11:10,675 --> 00:11:13,360
a similar message indicating

172
00:11:13,360 --> 00:11:15,685
that it has everything
that it needs,

173
00:11:15,685 --> 00:11:18,010
it agrees to all the parameters,

174
00:11:18,010 --> 00:11:22,030
has the key, and a summary of

175
00:11:22,030 --> 00:11:26,035
all of the messages it
understands to this point,

176
00:11:26,035 --> 00:11:30,415
again, ensuring integrity.

177
00:11:30,415 --> 00:11:35,140
At this point, communication
is now secured.

178
00:11:35,140 --> 00:11:37,900
We can now know that we're

179
00:11:37,900 --> 00:11:41,755
visiting the domain
that we're expecting.

180
00:11:41,755 --> 00:11:44,560
We're encrypting the traffic

181
00:11:44,560 --> 00:11:49,400
and all of that is
integrity checked.

182
00:11:49,980 --> 00:11:57,340
Now another thing
to know about TLS,

183
00:11:57,340 --> 00:11:58,480
so what we've just

184
00:11:58,480 --> 00:12:00,880
demonstrated is in
the most common case,

185
00:12:00,880 --> 00:12:04,150
it's going to be a
client needs to validate

186
00:12:04,150 --> 00:12:08,590
the authenticity of the
server that it's visiting.

187
00:12:08,590 --> 00:12:10,690
The server doesn't necessarily

188
00:12:10,690 --> 00:12:13,780
need to verify that I'm who I

189
00:12:13,780 --> 00:12:18,850
say I am because it doesn't
really know or care.

190
00:12:18,850 --> 00:12:23,455
But there are cases
where it does.

191
00:12:23,455 --> 00:12:28,510
This is where Mutual
TLS or MTLS comes in.

192
00:12:28,510 --> 00:12:30,820
As described, the
client verifying

193
00:12:30,820 --> 00:12:32,740
the identity of the
server is enough,

194
00:12:32,740 --> 00:12:34,600
that this domain of

195
00:12:34,600 --> 00:12:35,920
the public key matches

196
00:12:35,920 --> 00:12:39,580
the website that I'm
visiting as a client.

197
00:12:39,580 --> 00:12:42,130
Now if the server wants to

198
00:12:42,130 --> 00:12:44,410
verify the identity
of his clients,

199
00:12:44,410 --> 00:12:46,105
this could happen maybe it's for

200
00:12:46,105 --> 00:12:49,240
access control for
API endpoints,

201
00:12:49,240 --> 00:12:53,020
so I'm running some
service and I only want to

202
00:12:53,020 --> 00:12:58,045
let those that are my customers,

203
00:12:58,045 --> 00:13:02,155
that I've explicitly
allowed to use the APIs,

204
00:13:02,155 --> 00:13:05,515
and so I want to validate that.

205
00:13:05,515 --> 00:13:09,940
I want to validate they
are who they say they are.

206
00:13:09,940 --> 00:13:14,440
The browsers in the client
would look at the domain in

207
00:13:14,440 --> 00:13:17,080
the certificate and
match to what's

208
00:13:17,080 --> 00:13:19,885
being accessed, so
that's implicit,

209
00:13:19,885 --> 00:13:24,145
except all servers
would likely have

210
00:13:24,145 --> 00:13:27,010
an explicit access control list

211
00:13:27,010 --> 00:13:31,040
for what certificates
it's accepting.

212
00:13:37,350 --> 00:13:42,655
With that, we've
now covered TLS.

213
00:13:42,655 --> 00:13:46,125
That wraps up this module on

214
00:13:46,125 --> 00:13:50,220
network security where
we covered the basics,

215
00:13:50,220 --> 00:13:52,500
really try to tie
it into things we

216
00:13:52,500 --> 00:13:56,055
learned in other modules,

217
00:13:56,055 --> 00:13:58,140
and show how these
things all come

218
00:13:58,140 --> 00:14:02,070
together. With that, thanks.